mtd: physmap: use the exact reg size when there is no addr-gpio window

physmap computes the map size as BIT(fls64(reg_size) - 1) plus the
address-GPIO count, so a 7 MiB mtd-rom reg only exposes a 4 MiB
mtdblock0 and a rootfs larger than 4 MiB cannot be mounted.

The power-of-two rounding only matters for the addr-gpios windowing
scheme, where win_order selects a small aperture that the GPIO lines
slide across a larger chip.  With no addr-gpios the window is a plain
linear mapping and the MTD can expose the resource size exactly, so a
non-power-of-two partition keeps its whole length instead of being
truncated to the next lower power of two.

Signed-off-by: Devin <devin@cognition.ai>
---
 drivers/mtd/maps/physmap-core.c |   10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/mtd/maps/physmap-core.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/maps/physmap-core.c
@@ -513,9 +513,12 @@
 		if (!info->maps[i].phys)
 			info->maps[i].phys = res->start;
 
-		info->win_order = fls64(resource_size(res)) - 1;
-		info->maps[i].size = BIT(info->win_order +
-					 (info->gpios ?
-					  info->gpios->ndescs : 0));
+		/* win_order/BIT sizing only matters for the addr-gpios
+		 * aperture windowing; with no gpios expose the exact
+		 * resource size so a non-pow2 reg is not truncated. */
+		info->win_order = fls64(resource_size(res)) - 1;
+		info->maps[i].size = info->gpios
+			? BIT(info->win_order + info->gpios->ndescs)
+			: resource_size(res);
 
 		info->maps[i].map_priv_1 = (unsigned long)dev;
